# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A pragma stands only before the first block"

# TiddlyWiki reads pragmas in a mode of its own. wikiparser.js runs parsePragmas() once, before
# parseBlocks(), and never returns to it — so a backslash directive standing after any block content
# parses as ordinary text, and an author who writes one there sees it render as prose.
#
# The grammar reads the zone the same way. `#pragma-zone` opens at the start of the source and
# nowhere else — vscode-textmate compiles \A to something unmatchable on every line but the first,
# so `allowA` reads `isFirstLine`, and \G stands beside it for a caller opening the grammar at a
# line of its own, the way a .tid body begins where its header ends. It closes, zero-width, at the
# first line that opens no pragma-mode rule.
#
# EIGHT RULES, not the backslash family alone. TiddlyWiki names them itself — commentblock,
# fnprocdef, import, macrodef, parameters, parsermode, rules, whitespace — so an HTML comment holds
# the zone open exactly as a directive does. Reading the backslash family alone closed the zone on
# the first `<!-- -->` line of a sample and took fifty-four pragmas with it. `npm run rule-coverage`
# holds the grammar to the harvested set.
#
# THIS FILE CAN ONLY ASSERT THE NEGATIVE HALF. A fixture declaring the base scope reads under it,
# and the mandatory `# SYNTAX TEST` header reads as a numbered list — content, which closes the
# zone on line 1. So no pragma colours anywhere here, and the assertions below hold for a reason
# beside the one they name. The positive half — a pragma at the top of a body colouring in full —
# stands pinned in tests/samples/tiddlywiki5.pragma.tw.snap, and the fixtures that assert pragma
# sub-tokenization declare the wrapper scope `text.html.tiddlywiki5.test`, whose container carries
# the pragma rules, since a fixture stands as a fixture rather than as a tiddler.

Ordinary prose stands here.

\define greet(name)
#<- - punctuation.definition.directive.tiddlywiki5
hello $name$
\end

\rules except html
#<- - punctuation.definition.directive.tiddlywiki5
